## 6. Fit and cost

- Stack: a generic library release with semver tags. That fits projects which tag releases, but those projects' own conventions and the selected delivery path already govern how they release.
- Human-in-the-loop assumptions: the skill assumes one operator who may choose the version (ext SKILL.md:12) and push tags (ext SKILL.md:14) with no approval step. Firstmate's autonomous workers may do neither without the delivery path and the captain (section 5).
- Tool availability: `which git gh gh-axi no-mistakes pi` found all of them. The referenced `test-driven-development` skill is not on the surface, and the referenced URL does not resolve.
- Context cost: about 27 tokens of description on every session at `auto`, and about 161 tokens per body load. The cost is small. The problem is what the skill says, not its size.
- Who would load it: at trial-only placement, only the session that is given the path. In one project's `.agents/skills`, every worker in that project. At user-global `~/.agents/skills` plus the `~/.claude/skills` symlink, every Pi and Claude worker on the machine, auto-invoked on any release-flavoured task. In firstmate's `.agents/skills`, firstmate and all crewmates. Every placement wider than trial-only would expose autonomous workers to the ungated tag push.

## 7. Recommendation

Decline as `conflict`. The facts that settle it:
1. The only concrete action, ext SKILL.md:14, is a remote tag push that clashes with the selected delivery path (AGENTS.md:230, AGENTS.md:238, bin/fm-brief.sh:705) and with the rule that irreversible actions need an explicit captain instruction (AGENTS.md:427).
2. Nothing is worth harvesting:
   - ext SKILL.md:10 and :12 point at a file that does not exist and a domain that cannot resolve.
   - ext SKILL.md:11 is already covered by AGENTS.md:230.
   - ext SKILL.md:13 is one generic changelog sentence. It is project convention with no firstmate owner to put it in, far below the "more than a paragraph" bar.
3. Two independent facts would also block adoption: there is no license (copying is not authorized), and a copy placed anywhere would not bring along its checklist, its sibling skill or a working guide (section 2).

If the captain wants release help in firstmate, that is a separate task to write it from scratch. Such a task would gate the tag push behind the delivery path and an explicit captain instruction, and would rely on each project's own release conventions. It would borrow nothing from this text.

